# Kasha Varo, who sweeps the shrine yard of the White Sun and sings to the pool (docs/voice/kasha_varo.md).
# She knows the pool's moods, the graves around it, and what she's seen in the water over sixty years.

~ start
if flag.pool_offerings_taken and not flag.kasha_scolded
set kasha_scolded
Kasha Varo [angry]: Somebody's been in the pool after the wishes. Sixty years of wishes. I hope it was worth nine gold to be the sort of person who does that. No, don't tell me. The pool knows, and the pool doesn't gossip. I do.
-> menu
endif
if not flag.kasha_met
set kasha_met
Narrator: A tiny old woman in three shawls is sweeping snow off the stones around the pool with a broom taller than she is. She is singing to the water, tunelessly and with great affection.
Kasha Varo: Mind the edge. The pool doesn't like boots. Kasha Varo, if you're asking. You weren't, but people ought to, so I tell them anyway.
if guest:ireena
Narrator: She stops sweeping. She looks at Ireena for a long moment, and then, very slowly, makes the sign of the sun over her own heart.
Kasha Varo: You've been here before, girl. Long before me. Don't ask me how I know; my knees know things, and I've stopped arguing with them.
endif
else
Kasha Varo: Back again. The pool remembers you. It remembers everybody, but it's polite about it.
endif
-> menu

~ menu
* What is this place? -> place
* What's special about the pool? -> pool
* Whose graves are these? -> graves
* [if treasure_at:krezk_pool_of_the_white_sun] Have you seen anything strange in the water lately? -> strange
* [if flag.ireena_pool_vision] Have you ever seen a young man in the water? -> young_man
* [if flag.abbot_fate] The Abbot. Have you heard? -> abbot_after
* Do you keep any healing here? Potions, or prayers? -> wares
* Goodbye, Kasha. -> END

~ wares
Kasha Varo: Some. The pool's water goes into the draughts, and the prayers I write out on the long nights. They're not free. The shrine needs a new roof, and I need new knees, and only one of those can be bought.
shop
-> menu

~ place
Kasha Varo: The shrine of the White Sun. The morning lord, in Vallaki they'd say, but we're older than Vallaki and we call him what our grandmothers did. Krezk was built round this pool, not the other way.
Kasha Varo: The wall's for the wolves. The pool's for us.
-> menu

~ pool
Kasha Varo: It doesn't freeze. Never has. Snow falls in and stops being snow. And when the sun goes behind the clouds, look in it quick, and you'll see the sun stays in the water a breath longer than it stays in the sky.
Kasha Varo: It remembers, you see. The sun used to come here properly. The water's been holding onto it ever since, the way you'd keep a letter.
if name:kip_smudgewick
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Does it do anything? Heal? Bless? Could it be bottled? Asking professionally.
else
interject tag:curious: Does it do anything? Heal? Bless?
endif
Kasha Varo: Fill a flask and find out. It doesn't do anything for people who ask what it does. That's my experience. Sixty years of it.
-> menu

~ graves
Kasha Varo: Krezk's. All of us end up round the pool, sooner or later. The little stones by the pavilion are the burgomaster's. Five of them. Anna comes and sits with them on Tuesdays.
if flag.abbot_bride_revealed or flag.vasilka_examined or flag.turned_earth_found
Kasha Varo [afraid]: And some of them are lighter than they should be, if you take my meaning. I've found earth turned that I didn't turn. I put it back. I say a prayer. It's all I can do.
if name:thistle
interject name:thistle: The Abbot's bride. That's where he dug her up.
else
interject tag:blunt: The Abbot's bride. That's where he got her.
endif
Kasha Varo: I didn't say that. I'd never say that. Not out loud, where the mountain can hear.
endif
if not quest.lighter_than_it_should_be
set krezk_toys_rumor
quest lighter_than_it_should_be rumored
Kasha Varo: And somebody's been leaving toys on the Krezkov stones. Every morning this month. Carved. I leave them be. Toys aren't my business; graves are.
elif not flag.sorin_fate
Kasha Varo: The toys? Still coming. I've stopped sweeping round them. The eldest stone's always had the most. The eldest was always lighter than it should be, if you ask me. Nobody asks me.
endif
-> menu

~ strange
Kasha Varo: Since the autumn there's something down in the middle that shines when the sun's gone in. Not a coin. Coins don't shine like that.
Kasha Varo: I don't go in after it. My knees are the oldest residents of Krezk and they've earned their rest. You've young knees. Wade in, if you want it.
-> menu

~ young_man
Kasha Varo: Once. When I was a girl, and once again the winter my husband died. A young man in old clothes, looking up out of the water as if he'd lost something.
Kasha Varo: I thought he was a saint. Now I've seen your red-haired girl look at him, I think he's only someone waiting. Which is holier, in the end.
-> menu

~ abbot_after
if flag.abbot_fate == "repentant"
Kasha Varo: A light went up off the mountain at dawn. I stood here and watched it go and I didn't know whether to cheer or cry, so I swept. It's what I do with things I don't understand.
elif flag.abbot_fate == "slain"
Kasha Varo [sad]: Dead, they say. Something that was sent here to look after us, and went wrong, and you put it right the only way you could. I'll light it a candle. Somebody ought to.
elif flag.abbot_fate == "broken"
Kasha Varo: Sitting in his chapel, the Belviews say, not speaking. Well. I've sat by this pool not speaking for sixty years, and it's done me no harm. Maybe he'll come to something.
else
Kasha Varo [angry]: A cart went by in the night, they say, with a bride in it. I don't want to hear about it. I'll pray for her. She's ours, whatever she's made of.
endif
-> menu
